    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_ad1ef033d6cd7b6df8b0eb51f5b0365e7864b96b3a400734ed3161801a7a88fd",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Dirichlet-polynomial arguments are engineered to meet the scale of the available average theorem. In the zero-density chain, functional-equation reflection yields a dual finite contribution and remainder, correlation and moment estimates control large values, and powering moves a zero-detecting witness into an admissible length window. A mollifier uses a different construction: approximate inversion creates an exact initial coefficient gap and a short polynomial whose family norm is controlled by the large sieve.",
