    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_dd9e45022c5f7486e0a36c21024fb7728ffca1c7045f1ee9c160e710b8c6b57f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Forming a conductor- or parameter-varying L-function family changes the estimand from complete control of one zero set to a population observable. In quadratic families, large-sieve and density methods can then prove positive-proportion nonvanishing, derivative-nonvanishing counts, or aggregate analytic-rank bounds, and can exclude the scenario that every sufficiently large member has an exceptional real zero without resolving members individually.",
